    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and it passes. \n\n## Summary\n\nThe change rejects a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with HTTP 400.\n\n**Approach \u2014 kept structural impact low:**\n\n1. **`OwnerRegistrationDatePolicy.java`** \u2014 Added the rule inside the existing small, cohesive policy class (its `WMC_other` is tiny). Extracted the check into a new private helper `rejectFutureDate(...)` rather than growing the entry method, and added a nested `FutureRegistrationDateException` \u2014 mirroring the sibling `OwnerPostcodePolicy`/`InvalidPostcodeException` shape. The check runs before any registration-date-derived value is computed.\n\n2. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 Added one new `@ExceptionHandler` method mapping the exception to 400, matching how every other policy in this codebase surfaces its status. (The `@ResponseStatus` annotation alone can't work here because the advice's `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)` intercepts everything first \u2014 so the explicit handler is required for correctness.)\n\nI did **not** touch the large `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` method \u2014 the existing `assignBusinessDayRegistrationDate` call already routes through the policy, so the new rule is enforced with zero changes to that high-`WMC_other` controller. Two files touched, and every changed/added function is small with low surrounding complexity.\n\nBoth the new target test and the full suite are green.",
